name: gui-envsteal
description: Launch GUI apps and Wayland utilities from a background/systemd shell (opencode serve, telegram) by stealing the Hyprland session environment. Use when launching or controlling ANY GUI app, mpv, chromium, kitty, grim screenshots, notify-send, wtype, hyprctl, wallpaper/IPC tools, or when a launched app "silently never appears" or hyprctl fails with HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE not set.
---

# GUI launch from background shell: env-steal

## Why
opencode serve spawns at boot via systemd BEFORE Hyprland exists → its env has NO
`WAYLAND_DISPLAY`, `DISPLAY`, or `HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE`. It does have
`XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` + `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS` (systemd user session). Apps launched
bare from there are blind: they fail to connect to Wayland and die silently, or run
without a window. Rule 4b in AGENTS.md is the command; this skill is the full map.

## The essential variable set (steal ALL of these)
Full list found in a live GUI process (quickshell/waybar/nautilus/kitty):

```
XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=...       # socket dir; PipeWire/wlroots/SIG resolution breaks without it
WAYLAND_DISPLAY=...       # usually wayland-1; native Wayland apps (grim, wl-copy, sway tools)
DISPLAY=:0                # XWayland; needed by X clients + many Electron/GTK fallbacks
HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE=...   # hyprctl, omarchy-shell IPC, socket path
DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=...      # notifications, portals, gsettings (client), MPRIS
XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP / XDG_SESSION_DESKTOP / XDG_SESSION_TYPE   # most say Hyprland/wayland
XDG_SEAT=seat0 XDG_VTNR=1          # ID of seat/vt; some apps sanity-check
USER HOME LOGNAME PATH             # identity + binary lookup
GDK_BACKEND=wayland,x11,*          # GTK app routing (absent → auto, usually ok)
QT_QPA_PLATFORM=wayland;xcb        # Qt app routing (quickshell env carries this)
ELECTRON_OZONE_PLATFORM_HINT=wayland  # Electron apps route to wayland
MOZ_ENABLE_WAYLAND=1               # Firefox family
```

Stealing MORE than the "obvious" 3 vars is what made mpv / chromium work every time:
platform hints (`GDK_BACKEND`, `QT_QPA_PLATFORM`, `ELECTRON_OZONE_PLATFORM_HINT`,
`MOZ_ENABLE_WAYLAND`) and `XDG_SEAT`/`XDG_VTNR` are present in the GUI process env so
they get carried over automatically — don't hand-filter them out.

## The command template
```
src=$(pgrep -x quickshell | head -1 || pgrep -f "[w]aybar" | head -1 || pgrep -x kitty | head -1)
env -i $(tr '\0' '\n' < /proc/$src/environ | grep -E '^(XDG_RUNTIME_DIR|WAYLAND_DISPLAY|DISPLAY|HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE|DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS|XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP|XDG_SESSION_DESKTOP|XDG_SESSION_TYPE|XDG_SEAT|XDG_VTNR|USER|HOME|LOGNAME|PATH|GDK_BACKEND|QT_QPA_PLATFORM|ELECTRON_OZONE_PLATFORM_HINT|MOZ_ENABLE_WAYLAND)=') \
  setsid -f mpv --force-window=yes --title="X" "url" >/tmp/app.log 2>&1
```
`setsid -f <app> & disown` returns instantly; plain `setsid` HANGS the tool ~120s.
Never launch the GUI app in a command-substitution (`$(...)`) with a `&` child that
holds the stdout pipe open — the substitution waits forever (hit today: `wl-copy &
wl-paste` froze the shell tool past timeout).

## Failure signature cheat-sheet (VERIFIED today)
| Symptom (exact string) | Root cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE not set! (is hyprland running?)` | SIG missing | steal SIG |
| `Couldn't connect to /run/user/1000/hypr/STALE/.socket.sock. (4)` | SIG is STALE (old value, not "not set") | rediscover: `ls /run/user/1000/hypr/` → dir name = current SIG |
| `Could not connect to PipeWire` (wpctl/pactl rc=2) | XDG_RUNTIME_DIR missing | steal XDG_RUNTIME_DIR |
| `failed to create display` (grim rc=1) / `Failed to connect to Wayland display` | WAYLAND_DISPLAY missing | steal WAYLAND_DISPLAY |
| App runs but window silently never appears | WAYLAND_DISPLAY or SIG missing, or wrong workspace | steal env THEN verify `hyprctl clients` |
| `notify-send` rc=0 but no bubble appears | DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS missing → SILENT FAIL (rc lies!) | steal DBUS; verify via `dbus-monitor "interface=org.freedesktop.Notifications"` saw `member=Notify` |
| `vaInitialize failed: unknown libva` (chromium) | HW-accel libva missing — BENIGN, app still works | ignore |
| `cat: /proc/NNN/environ: No such file or directory` (empty) | source process died | re-pick live GUI pid |
| `class: mpv` present but no icon/sound | fine — check volume, not env | `wpctl get-volume` / `pactl list sink-inputs` |

## Verified behaviors (today)
- `gsettings get` WORKS even WITHOUT DBUS (dconf has a fallback channel). Do not use
  gsettings to detect missing DBUS.
- `hyprctl instances` → prints current instance id + pid; good cross-check that SIG is right.
- `hyprctl workspaces/clients -j` → clean JSON only work with correct SIG + XDG_RUNTIME_DIR.
- `wl-copy` keeps a pipe held open while it owns the clipboard — combine with
  `timeout 3 wl-copy` and a separate `timeout 3 wl-paste` invocation; never background both.
- `xdg-open file.txt` opens the terminal editor (nvim) — app picker honors session defaults.
- Env home: when the GUI pid you pick is a compositor/heavy shell (quickshell), it
  carries the COMPLETE correct set. Prefer quickshell > waybar > kitty > nautilus.

## IME gotcha
If the stolen `GTK_IM_MODULE` is set, GTK apps launched this way get a double IM
context and swallow input (Vietnamese/Teochew/etc). Do NOT carry `GTK_IM_MODULE`
into launches of GTK apps — see the `fcitx-ime` skill for the full diagnosis.

## Rediscover after reboot
- `HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE` changes every boot.
- Get it: `ls /run/user/1000/hypr/` → the single dir name IS the signature. Or
  `hyprctl instances` (needs SIG to query — chicken/egg; use `ls`).
- `/tmp` (venv `/tmp/cdp-venv`, scripts) is wiped on reboot — recreate.

## Verify before declaring success
1. `hyprctl clients` (with stolen env) shows the expected `class:`/`title:`.
2. For audio: `pactl list sink-inputs` shows the app stream, not muted; sink not muted.
3. For browser: `curl -s localhost:9222/json` lists the page target.
Screenshot via `grim -o eDP-2 /tmp/x.png` + telegram sendPhoto for visual proof.
--- WM OPS ---
---
name: wm-ops
description: Direct Hyprland/Oarchy window-manager operations from a shell — switch/move workspaces, focus/move/close windows, verify placement. Use when asked to put windows on a workspace, drag a window, switch workspaces, close/focus a specific app window, or inspect where windows live. Distinct from launching apps (gui-envsteal) and browser control (chromium-cdp).
---

# Hyprland WM operations (Omarchy)

From a background shell (opencode serve / telegram) there is NO Hyprland env — set SIG + XDG_RUNTIME_DIR first, always:
```
export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000 WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-1 HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE=$(ls /run/user/1000/hypr/)
```
SIG rediscovered dynamically via `ls /run/user/1000/hypr/` (changes each boot).

## Workspaces — Omarchy uses Lua DSL, legacy syntax FAILS
`hyprctl dispatch workspace 2` → Lua parse error `')' expected near '2'`. Use DSL:
```
hyprctl dispatch 'hl.dsp.focus({ workspace = "2" })'       # switch to workspace
hyprctl dispatch 'hl.dsp.focus({ workspace = "previous" })' # go back to former ws
hyprctl activeworkspace | grep "workspace ID"              # verify (needs SIG)
```

## Window ops (move / focus / close)
```
addr=$(hyprctl -j clients | python3 -c "
import json,sys
for c in json.load(sys.stdin):
    if (c.get('class') or '').lower()=='chromium': print(c['address'])" | head -1)
hyprctl dispatch 'hl.dsp.focus({ window = "address:'"$addr"'" })'      # focus window by address
hyprctl dispatch 'hl.dsp.window.move({ workspace = "3" })'             # move focused window to ws 3
hyprctl dispatch 'hl.dsp.window.move({ workspace = "3", follow = false })'  # move silently (don't jump there)
hyprctl dispatch 'hl.dsp.window.close()'                               # close focused window
```
Verify placement after each op:
```
hyprctl -j clients | python3 -c "
import json,sys
for c in json.load(sys.stdin):
    if (c.get('class') or '').lower()=='<class>':
        print(c['address'], c.get('workspace',{}).get('id'))"
```
NOTE (verified 2026-09-11): `hl.dsp.focus({window=addr})` JUMPS to that window's workspace. So the sequence focus-then-move(`follow=false`) leaves you on the ORIGINAL workspace (e.g. focus chrome on ws3, move to ws4 follow=false → you end back on ws3). To end on the destination, switch there FIRST, then move.

## App launch lands on ACTIVE workspace
A newly launched app opens on whichever workspace is focused right now. So:
1. `hyprctl dispatch 'hl.dsp.focus({ workspace = "3" })'`
2. launch the app (see gui-envsteal skill)
3. verify with `hyprctl -j clients` → workspace.id

## Kill-by-name safely (self-kill trap)
`pkill -f "chrom"`, `pkill -f "[c]hromium"` MATCH the tool's own bash command line if the SAME command contains any other literal `chromium` string (e.g. `pgrep -x chromium`) → SUICIDES the shell → looks like a timeout/env bug. Rules:
- Kill in a SOLO command: `pkill -f "chromiu[mX]"` alone, no other command referencing the name in the same line.
- Or `kill -9 $(pgrep -x name)` in a separate command with no other occurrence.
- After kill: `ss -tln | grep 9222` for ports, and clean state dirs (`/tmp/.org.chromium.Chromium.*`, stale IPC sockets) before relaunch.

## Quick checks
- `hyprctl clients` / `hyprctl -j clients` (clean JSON only with correct SIG), `hyprctl activeworkspace`, `hyprctl monitors`.
